ERROR REPORTS

We don't process error reports (see note on top of this file).

BUILDING AND RUNNING XV6

To build xv6 on an x86 ELF machine (like Linux or FreeBSD), run
"make". On non-x86 or non-ELF machines (like OS X, even on x86), you
will need to install a cross-compiler gcc suite capable of producing
x86 ELF binaries (see https://pdos.csail.mit.edu/6.828/).
Then run "make TOOLPREFIX=i386-jos-elf-". Now install the QEMU PC
simulator and run "make qemu".

------------------------------------------------------------------------
EDF SCHEDULER & PRIORITY DONATION (PERSON 2: ANHAD)
------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. SCHEDULER MODES:
   - Mode 0 (SCHED_RR): Original Round-Robin scheduler (default).
   - Mode 1 (SCHED_EDF): Earliest Deadline First without priority donation.
   - Mode 2 (SCHED_EDF_DONATE): EDF with Transitive Priority Donation.

2. BUILD COMMANDS:
   - Round-Robin:
     make SCHED_MODE=0
   - EDF without donation:
     make SCHED_MODE=1
   - EDF with Priority Donation:
     make SCHED_MODE=2
   Switching SCHED_MODE rebuilds everything automatically (the Makefile
   tracks the last mode in .sched_mode), so "make clean" is not required.
   - Multiprocessor configuration:
     make qemu-nox CPUS=1
     make qemu-nox CPUS=3

3. HOW EDF WORKS:
   The scheduler inspects all RUNNABLE processes and prioritizes the
   process with the earliest absolute deadline. Processes without deadlines
   (NO_DEADLINE = 0) have lowest priority and are scheduled round-robin
   when no deadline tasks are runnable. Ties among equal-deadline processes
   (and among no-deadline processes) are resolved using a rotating cursor,
   so equal-priority processes share the CPU.

   Limitation: like any pure EDF scheduler this is not starvation-free
   across priority levels. While a CPU-bound process with a deadline is
   runnable, processes without one (including sh) get no CPU on that core,
   and a process that misses its deadline keeps its priority until it
   sets a new one or exits.

4. HOW PRIORITY DONATION WORKS:
   When a process A blocks waiting on process B, B temporarily inherits
   A's effective deadline urgency:
     D_effective(P) = min(D_own(P), D_donated(P))
   Donations propagate transitively across dependency chains (e.g.,
   Stage 4 -> Stage 3 -> Stage 2 -> Stage 1) via bounded relaxation.
   Cycles are detected and rejected. Multiple donors are resolved by taking
   the minimum deadline across all active donors, and donations are cleanly
   recomputed upon dependency withdrawal, process exit, and reaping.

   Kernel API (defs.h): proc_set_waiting_on_pid(owner_pid) makes the
   current process wait on owner_pid; proc_clear_waiting_on(myproc())
   withdraws it. Call set before sleep() and clear after it returns, and
   never while holding ptable.lock. Owners must be live (not EMBRYO or
   ZOMBIE).

5. HOW TO EXECUTE TESTS:
   Within xv6 shell:
     $ edftest
     $ workflowtest
     $ pipeline_test
   edftest adapts to the mode it was built with: Scenario 2 (EDF order) is
   skipped under SCHED_MODE=0, and the donation scenarios expect each
   process's own deadline under modes 0 and 1 (donation is recorded but
   not applied) and the donated deadline under mode 2.
